From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 15:28:33 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] print: the nozzle guard's pause line in the screen's language

Kiln's nozzle-guard plugin (patch 0098 has its setting) writes one
English line before it pauses the print:

  !! Nozzle hit detected: the bed felt 412 g (limit 300 g) at Z 1.20.
  Print paused: check the print and the nozzle, then resume or cancel.

(" at Z ..." left out when the position is unknown). That line was the
pause reason on the print status screen and the error dialog's text, so
it showed in English whatever the screen's language.

kiln::nozzle_hit_reason() (kiln_nozzle_hit_reason.cpp) recognises the
line, reads the force, the limit and Z as Klipper wrote them, and gives
"Nozzle hit: the bed felt {} g (limit {} g) at Z {}. Check the print and
the nozzle, then resume or cancel." in the screen's language, without
the Z part when the line has none. A line it can't read gives "Nozzle
hit detected. Check the print and the nozzle, then resume or cancel.";
so does a translation whose placeholders don't fit. Any other line is
shown as before.

It runs on the main thread where the text is shown: in
PrintStopReasonTracker::publish() for the pause reason (also a reason
read back from the gcode store), and in GcodeErrorRouter::process_line()
for the error dialog, where raw_detail keeps Klipper's wording for the
dedup. The reason is translated when the print pauses; a language change
while paused shows the new language at the next publish.

The three sentences are in all eleven catalogs (en and de, es, fr, it,
ja, ko, pt, ru, tr, zh), with the words patch 0098's translations use
for the guard; the ja and zh text uses only characters the built-in CJK
fonts have.
